<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dubai Creek</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dubai <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> is a saltwater inlet that divides Dubai into Deira and Bur Dubai. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-bu-falasah">Al Bu Falasah</a> settled on its banks in 1833, and its sheltered harbour made Dubai a centre of pearling and trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Its dredging in 1959–1961 under Sheikh Rashid bin Saeed allowed larger ships to enter and launched Dubai's growth as a port.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dubai-creek.jpg" alt="Dubai Creek" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Phil6007 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_view_of_Dubai_Creek.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dubai <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> is a natural saltwater inlet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> that extends about 14 kilometres inland and forms a natural port long used for trade and transport. It ranges from 200 to 1,200 metres in width, with an average depth of 6.5 to 7 metres. It ends naturally at the Ras Al Khor Wildlife Sanctuary. Abras, small boats, have traditionally carried people across it, and the two sides are now linked by bridges and a tunnel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The creek divided the city into Bur Dubai and Deira. Members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bani-yas">Bani Yas</a> tribe first settled along the Bur Dubai side in the 19th century and established the Al Maktoum dynasty there. In the early 20th century it could not take large ships, which the current also impeded, and served as a minor port for dhows from as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>. It was nonetheless the only harbour in the city and the basis of Dubai's commercial position. The pearling industry, the main sector of the economy until cultured pearls were invented in the 1930s, was based on expeditions from the creek. Fishing also centred on it, and fishing dhows were built on its foreshore.</p><p>In 1955 a plan was made to develop the creek by dredging shallow areas, building breakwaters and turning the beach into a quay for cargo. After the first dredging, vessels of 7-foot (2.1 m) draught could cross at all times, and further dredging in the 1960s and 1970s allowed anchorage for coastal shipping of up to about 500 tons. Traffic became continuous, re-export developed, and Dubai gained an advantage over Sharjah, then the other main trading centre of the region. The Al Maktoum Bridge, the first to join Bur Dubai and Deira, was built in 1963.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The creek lost importance as a port with the growth of Jebel Ali Port, though Port Saeed and other small facilities still serve traders from the region and the subcontinent. In September 2007 an extension costing 484 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a> was finished, and the Dubai Water Canal, a final 2.2-kilometre stretch inaugurated on 9 November 2016, took the waterway through Business Bay and Jumeirah to the Gulf, adding about 13 kilometres. Landmarks on its banks include the Deira Twin Towers, Dubai Creek Park and the Dubai Creek Golf &amp; Yacht Club.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dubai Creek” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1833 about 800 members of the Al Bu Falasah section of the Bani Yas left the Sheikhdom of Abu Dhabi and settled at <b>Dubai Creek</b> under Maktoum bin Butti and Obaid bin Said.</blockquote><a href="/state/sheikhdom-of-dubai">Sheikhdom of Dubai</a></li></ul></section>
